e sorry that um a process that  has failed you that did not give you the  comfort that you could  come forward and you know without the  fear of reprisal  something that we are absolutely  committed to fixing and we will  survivors tell me some are skeptical but  they are relieved to see the government  taking action there are still questions  though about whether  more should have been done in 2018.  that's when the military ombudsman at  the time told defense minister sajin of  an allegation of inappropriate behavior  by former chief of the defense staff  general jonathan vance  vance has denied all allegations he  acted inappropriately  the prime minister has said no one in  his office knew the complaint  was a quote metoo complaint but global  news has seen documents showing  bureaucrats were told the allegation  involved sexual harassment  and so i pushed the minister on what  exactly  he knew about that complaint were you  aware that it was sexual in nature or  had to do with conduct around women  we'll be honest that's that's something  that as i stated  um i didn't have much details um  on that um i wanted to my my priority  was to making sure that the former  ombudsman  was able to then take it immediately uh  and speak to the privy council  office about this so that the  appropriate action  could be taken also on the show this  week i'll speak with health minister  patty haidu on whether her government is  doing enough to protect canadians  from a crippling third wave of covid19  robin all right mercedes-stevenson in  ottawa thanks mercedes  you can see the interviews with defense  minister harjeet sudjan and health  minister patty haidu tomorrow on the  west block right here on global still  ahead a bombing leaves afghanistan in  anguish  at least 27 people were killed and more  than 100 others  injured in a suicide bombing in eastern  afghanistan  high school students are among the  casualties they were staying at a guest  house  preparing to take university entrance  exams when a truck packed with  explosives  was detonated outside the home the  seriously wounded were taken to  hospitals in the country's capital  kabul no group has claimed  responsibility yet  but the afghan government accuses the  taliban of carrying out the attack  afghanistan is bracing for more violence  as the u.s begins withdrawing its troops  from the country  about 3 500 american soldiers are still  there  president joe biden says it is time to  put an end  to what has become known as the forever  war today was the day all foreign  soldiers were to leave the country under  an agreement between the taliban  and the former trump administration the  u.s now says  all of its military personnel will be  out of the country by september  11th it has been three months since the  military seized power in myanmar  [Applause]  and today demonstrators returned to the  streets demanding the return of the  democratically elected leaders  protesters aren't giving up in the face  of a relentless crackdown  which includes internet blackouts at  least 759 people have died since the  february coup  according to an advocacy group more than  3 400 people have been imprisoned  the united nations warns that without a  collective international response  the general administration of the  country could risk  coming to a standstill a marathon a day  will keep  one bc woman busy all may up next the  cause  behind the kilometers  you get a love bite on your neck your  life's going down the toilet  academy award-winning actress olympia  dukakis has died  dukakis won an oscar for best supporting  actress in 1988  for her role in moonstruck starring  opposite cher  she captivated audiences with her  performances in steel magnolias  tales of the city and several canadian  productions  including the 2011 film cloud burst  olympia dukakis was 89. running a  marathon may be a once-in-a-lifetime  goal but a bc woman is making it her  daily mission this month over the next  30 days jana hempler plans to run  1266 kilometers  just thinking about it is exhausting but  this runner says she'll get her energy  from the cause behind her course  kylie stanton explains i'm not getting  any younger so it's either now or never  most people would choose never but janna  hempler  is not most people i'm going to attempt  to run  30 marathons in 30 days sort of this  marathon spree if you want to call it  that  hempler has plenty of training under her  belt back in 2013 she ran the length of  vancouver  island in 2015 she qualified and  conquered the boston marathon  and just last year she committed to  running 12 marathons  in as many days raising money along the  way  it just lifted my spirits you know to  see people donating and supporting and  it was just under 36 000 for the  victoria hospitals foundation  now with even more miles to cover she's  hoping to raise even more money  500 000 dollars in total that once again  will go directly to the victoria  hospitals foundation  providing priority equipment research  and education needs to the two biggest  hospitals on vancouver island  it's a time right now where i know that  everyone needs a little bit more  inspiration  especially our care teams when they know  someone like jana is doing something so  powerful and so stressful itself  it certainly helps you know the care  teams see this  and they know people are giving back to  the hospitals and they are so grateful  this pandemic has been a marathon in  itself  and while hempler knows the next 30 days  will be tough  she says they'll pale in comparison to  what frontline workers are experiencing  at least her run has a finish line our  healthcare heroes they need more support  than ever before  and so this is my way of honoring them  kylie stanton global news victoria  good for her and that is global national  for this saturday night  i'm robin gill tonight your canada is  collins bay in kingston ontario  we would love to see your corner of the 